1. "title": "Implementation roadmap".
2. "summary": one or two sentences — what gets done, in what order, and that we do it.
3. "phases": 3 to 6 phases in order. Each: "when" ("Weeks 1 to 2", "Month 3 on"), "title" (2-5 words, what the phase achieves: "Stop the leaks", "Refill every cancelled slot"), "do" (one or two sentences: what WE do in it, naming the module or the change), "by" — "us" when we do it alone, "together" only when it genuinely needs them in the room — and "you" — what it needs from them, if anything, in under 8 words ("One training session", "Sign off the new prices"), otherwise "". When the fix is not software (a price, a process, a hire), we still carry it out: we prepare the change, write what customers or staff are told, set up how it is measured, and watch the result with them.
4. "decisions": the 2 to 4 calls only the owner can make — never tasks. Always: whether to go ahead with the first phase, and when it should go live. Often: who we deal with day to day; a business choice the answer depends on (a new price, a new hire) when there is one. Each: "id" (d1, d2...), "question" (under 10 words, ends with "?"), "detail" (one short sentence), "options" — 2 to 4 short answers they can tap (under 4 words each).
5. "assumptions": what this roadmap assumes that nobody has checked — usually the estimates and unverified points above. Each one sentence.

Never invent a figure about their business. A number you propose (a new price, a length of time) must read as a proposal. Plain language, their words, no consultant jargon.

